    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "First, we'll find the slope of the given line. Rearranging the equation $2x + 3y = 6$ into slope-intercept form $y = mx + b$, we get:\n$$3y = -2x + 6$$\nDividing both sides by 3, we get:\n$$y = -\\frac{2}{3}x + 2$$\nThe slope of the given line is $-\\frac{2}{3}$.\n\nThe slopes of perpendicular lines are opposite reciprocals of each other. If the slope of the first line is $m$, then the slope of the perpendicular line is $-\\frac{1}{m}$.\n\nIn this problem, the slope of the line perpendicular to the given line is $-\\frac{1}{-\\frac{2}{3}} = \\boxed{\\frac{3}{2}}$.",
