    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "First, convert the given equation to slope-intercept form $y = mx + b$.\n\\begin{align*}\n2x + 3y &= 6 \\\\\n3y &= -2x + 6 \\\\\ny &= -\\frac{2}{3}x + 2\n\\end{align*}\nThe slope of the given line is $-\\frac{2}{3}$.\nThe slope of the line perpendicular to it is the negative reciprocal of $-\\frac{2}{3}$, which is $\\boxed{\\frac{3}{2}}$.\n\nVisualize the problem, first.\n\nThe slope perpendicular to a line with a slope $m$ has $m$ as its negative reciprocal.\n\nVisual reference.\n\n[asy]\nunitsize(0.5 cm);\n\npair A = (3,1);\npair B = (1,5);\npair C = (3,-1);\n\nreal m = 135;\nreal BexA = (B.x - A.x)*tan(m);\nreal ByA = B.y - A.y;\n\ndraw((-2,0)--(8.2,0),Arrows(4));\ndraw((0,-2)--(0,4),Arrows(4));\n\ndraw(A--B--C--cycle);\n\nlabel(\"$x$\", (8,0), N);\nlabel(\"$y$\", (0,4), E);\n\nlabel(\"$A$\", A, NE);\nlabel(\"$B$\", B, NE);\nlabel(\"$C$\", C, SW);\nlabel(\"$m \\cdot BexA$\", (B.x+BexA,BexA),NE);\nlabel(\"$m \\cdot ByA$\", (ByA,BexA),NE);\n[/asy]\n\nExpand the explanation.\n\nThe y-intercept of a vertical line is infinity because it is a vertical line. This behavior suggests that the perpendicular line goes horizontally through the x-intercept of the given line. Therefore the perpendicular line does have a defined $y$-intercept. Therefore the slope of the perpendicular line is $0$.\n\n\nExpand the solution.\n\nThe slope of a line's perpendicular is the negative reciprocal of the line's slope. Since the slope of the given line is $-\\frac{2}{3}$, we have $-\\frac{3}{2} = \\frac{m}{1}$ for the perpendicular. This simplifies to $m = \\frac{3}{2}$.",
