      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "Step 1: Rewrite the equation into slope-intercept form. To do this, we need to isolate y on one side of the equation. \n\n\\[ 2x + 3y = 6 \\]\n\nDivide by 3 to get\n\n\\[ \\frac{2x}{3} + y = 2 \\]\n\nAnd then isolate the y term:\n\n\\[ y = 2 - \\frac{2}{3}x \\]\n\nWe now have the equation in the slope-intercept form, $y = mx + b$, where m is the slope and b is the y-intercept. In our case, m = $-\\frac{2}{3}$. \n\nStep 2: Determine the slope of the perpendicular line. The tangent inside the paper has a slope perpendicular to the tangent you showed is the negative reciprocal of your slope. Thus, the perpendicular slope, m\\_perpendicular = $-\\frac{3}{2}$.\n\nStep 3: Box the final solution. We'll put our result into $\\boxed{}$.\n\nSo, the solution is $m\\_perpendicular = \\boxed{-\\frac{3}{2}}$.",
